API and mechanism
src/import/claude.rs:132 — convert(BufRead + Send + 'static) returns Conversion,
a Stream<Item = Result<Envelope, ImportError>>. convert_with takes an optional
Set, blob_root and supplied-redactor-only option; metrics() exposes structural
counters. Configured capability/profile secrets are resolved before projection.
Sixteen-envelope preparation batches release their worker permit before async
delivery through the one-envelope channel. Paused consumers cannot occupy all
preparation workers; dropping a stream releases its owned reader.
src/import/mod.rs:10 and main.rs dispatch import claude SESSION.jsonl [-o FILE].
Stdout is the default. -o creates a new 0600 output file and sanitized text
sidecars under <output parent>/<session>/blobs/<digest>; it refuses overwrite.
Canonical JSON recursively sorts objects, including under ACP preserve_order.
time 0.3.55, already in Cargo.lock, is now a direct parsing dependency.

Mapping (also the module doc table)
| Claude Code input | Harness protocol |
| --- | --- |
| user text | UserMessage |
| assistant text | AgentMessage; large text split without byte loss |
| thinking / redacted_thinking | Reasoning / safe withheld-content Notice |
| tool_use + tool_result | paired ToolCall; explicit failure/interruption status |
| Bash | Terminal child with command, exit and sanitized output blob; live snapshot before completion |
| Edit / Write / MultiEdit | FileChange + TurnDiff; recorded structuredPatch positions/context, replacement-argument fallback when absent |
| TodoWrite | Plan entries/status/priority; large lists split into bounded items |
| image | Image MIME/size/hash metadata; no image bytes persisted |
| sidechain / Task / Agent | Subagent with a deterministic session identity |
| summary / compact_boundary / isCompactSummary | Compaction, summary blob and readable summary Notice |
| message usage | Usage with input/output/cache-read/reasoning counts when supplied |
| request / end_turn / max_tokens / EOF | TurnQueued/Started/Ended; unfinished tool results synthesized before ending |
| RFC3339/numeric timestamp | ts_ms; source instants retained, missing/invalid uses preceding instant or zero with invalid-value Notice |
| unknown/malformed/oversized/truncated | explicit Notice, never raw parser diagnostics |

src/import/claude/engine.rs:312,413,510,715,830 owns pending calls and source
projection. Duplicate/orphan calls and missing results receive explicit repairs.
A mixed result/new-request record pairs its old result before starting the new
turn. A per-line turn discriminator keeps a first-record orphan repair distinct
from that record's new request. IDs use stable hashes and ordinals, with no
randomness or wall clock. Golden comparisons prove repeatability with fixed
redaction configuration and across the default/ACP feature combinations.

Bounds and readable content
Raw lines are bounded at 1 MiB; oversized lines are drained and reported.
Pending state is capped at 32 tools and 4 MiB of retained argument/identity data.
The oldest pending call is explicitly closed when admission exceeds a budget.
Tool identities are hashed, so an unusually large provider ID cannot evade the
pending-byte budget. Structured arguments remain available for mapping even
when their display needs continuation cards. Input/output continuations retain
their text. Large diff display is an announced excerpt; full argument text stays
in continuation Notices. Well-formed metadata-only blob references are emitted without a
sidecar root; readable tool/summary text is also present in items.

tests/import_claude.rs:237 measured an actual 100,000,640-byte synthetic file:
12,136 lines; peak source line 8,240 bytes; peak emitted queue 3; delivery batch
16; pending bytes/tools 0. An independently observed BufRead consumed 65,920
bytes at the first delivered envelope, below the fixed batch read-ahead bound.
The complete result also passes check_transcript. Separate 1,000-call and
40 x 400,000-byte-argument tests exercise both count and byte admission limits,
with complete pairing at EOF. Six paused streams prove worker-permit release
and reader disposal. These are structural checks, without a latency percentile.
